// The dangerous face of prelude-name shadowing, as a fixture.
//
// A user type may take a prelude name; the reference removes the prelude
// declaration of that name, so the program means the USER's type. When the port
// did not, `struct String` kept the PRELUDE's shape - and because the prelude's
// String owns a heap buffer, the port also synthesised a drop for it. The
// emitted program then called `free_fn` on a pointer it never allocated, from a
// struct whose fields the user never declared.
//
// Both compilers ACCEPT this file. The assertion is therefore PARITY, not exit
// status: the emitted C must name the user's two-field struct and must contain
// no free at all. An exit-status check would have passed throughout.
//
// `String` is alone here on purpose. `Arena` and `StringBuilder` are registered
// by the same eager path, but the reference REJECTS shadowing them - its own
// prelude reads `Arena.off` - so they belong to the rejection class and are
// pinned there, not in an accept-parity file.

struct String { a: int, b: int }

fn width(s: *const String) -> int { return s.b - s.a; }

fn main() {
    let s = String { a: 3, b: 9 };
    println(width(&s));      // 6
}
